2026-06-14 16:09:07.571 INFO  - INPUT  {technique_id=T0851, technique_name=Rootkit, technique_description=Adversaries may deploy rootkits to hide the presence of programs, files, network connections, services, drivers, and other system components. Rootkits are programs that hide the existence of malware by intercepting and modifying operating-system API calls that supply system information. Rootkits or rootkit-enabling functionality may reside at the user or kernel level in the operating system, or lower. Firmware rootkits that affect the operating system yield nearly full control of the system. While firmware rootkits are normally developed for the main processing board, they can also be developed for the I/O that is attached to an asset. Compromise of this firmware allows the modification of all of the process variables and functions the module engages in., control_name=Physical enclosures, control_description=Physical enclosures refer to securing critical hardware, such as Programmable Logic Controllers (PLCs), Remote Terminal Units (RTUs), and operational network switches within locked, tamper-resistant cabinets or control panels. These enclosures are designed to prevent unauthorised personnel from gaining direct physical access to sensitive equipment, preventing attackers from bypassing network-level firewalls, manipulating physical processes, or triggering disruptive downtime. Beyond mitigating malicious tampering and accidental human error, industrial enclosures also provide environmental protection.}
2026-06-14 16:09:12.381 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":2,"coverage_justification":"Physical enclosures provide limited deterrence against rootkit deployment in ICS environments. The control directly addresses one specific vector for rootkit installation: preventing physical access to firmware programming interfaces on critical devices (PLCs, RTUs, I/O modules). By restricting unauthorized physical access to these assets, the control can deter firmware-based rootkit installation through direct physical tampering or JTAG/debug port access, which represents a meaningful but narrow subset of rootkit deployment vectors.\n\nHowever, the control has significant limitations. Rootkits can be deployed through multiple pathways that physical enclosures do not address: (1) network-based exploitation of vulnerable services or protocols, (2) supply chain compromises where rootkits are pre-installed in firmware before deployment, (3) malicious insiders with legitimate physical or logical access, (4) exploitation of remote management interfaces, and (5) lateral movement from compromised systems. Physical enclosures only deter the unauthorized physical access vector, not the substantially larger attack surface involving network access, social engineering, or legitimate credentials.\n\nThe control is architecturally relevant to firmware rootkit threats specifically, making it more than merely defense-in-depth, but it leaves the majority of practical rootkit deployment vectors available to attackers. An unskilled attacker using network-based methods would bypass this control entirely. A skilled attacker with legitimate access credentials or network pathways would not be meaningfully deterred. The control would only effectively deter opportunistic attacks targeting firmware through direct physical tampering at unlocked equipment, which represents a minority scenario in mature industrial environments."}
