2026-06-14 18:35:51.207 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":3,"coverage_justification":"User security training directly addresses the human element that is central to the spearphishing attachment technique. The control's effectiveness operates through multiple mechanisms: (1) training users to recognize spearphishing indicators such as suspicious sender addresses, unusual requests, and social engineering tactics; (2) educating users about the dangers of opening unsolicited attachments; (3) ICS-specific training that emphasizes operational security and threat awareness; and (4) protocols around removable media and credential management that reduce lateral movement opportunities if initial compromise occurs.\n\nThe control deters approximately 50-75% of spearphishing attachment vectors by significantly increasing the likelihood that trained users will identify and reject malicious emails before opening attachments. This training is particularly effective against less sophisticated spearphishing campaigns and moderately skilled attackers who rely on general phishing templates.\n\nHowever, the control has limitations that prevent a higher rating: (1) Determined and skilled attackers can craft highly convincing, targeted spearphishing emails with social engineering that may bypass even well-trained users; (2) The control is dependent on consistent user adherence and awareness, which degrades over time without continuous reinforcement; (3) Zero-day attachments or novel malware that exploits previously unknown vulnerabilities may not be recognizable to users regardless of training; (4) A single user lapse or highly sophisticated phishing campaign can defeat the control. The control is human-behavior dependent rather than providing technical deterrence, making it vulnerable to determined, well-resourced adversaries who employ advanced social engineering, spear-phishing with personalized reconnaissance, or exploits for which training cannot prepare users."}
