2026-06-14 18:36:21.259 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":2,"coverage_justification":"User security training provides behavioral deterrence against T0895 (Autorun Image) by educating operators about risks associated with removable media and establishing organizational protocols against their use. The control specifically addresses the human element by training users to recognize and avoid inserting unvetted USB drives or disk images into critical systems, which directly targets the attack vector of executing code via AutoRun functionality on removable media.\n\nHowever, the control has significant limitations: (1) It relies on human compliance and judgment, which is inherently unreliable—even well-trained users may make mistakes or face social engineering pressure. (2) It provides no technical prevention; a determined attacker can still exploit AutoRun if a user is manipulated or coerced into using removable media. (3) The control does not address scenarios where AutoRun is technically enabled on systems—it only discourages the insertion of media, not the underlying vulnerability. (4) Advanced attackers can bypass this through sophisticated social engineering, insider threats, or supply chain compromises that introduce malicious media in trusted contexts.\n\nThe control deters casual or opportunistic attacks and unskilled attackers who rely on user negligence, but does not fundamentally prevent the technique's execution if an attacker successfully manipulates a user into enabling AutoRun or inserting malicious media. It addresses perhaps 25-40% of typical attack vectors in OT environments, primarily those dependent on user behavior rather than technical exploitation. A rating of 2 reflects that this is an important but insufficient standalone control that requires complementary technical mitigations (such as disabling AutoRun at the system level or air-gapping critical systems)."}
