2026-06-14 18:36:59.103 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":2,"coverage_justification":"User security training provides limited deterrence against Valid Accounts (T0859) attacks. The control addresses credential compromise through social engineering awareness (phishing, pretexting) and can help users recognize when to protect credentials or avoid sharing them, which may deter some credential theft vectors. It can also help operators recognize anomalous system behavior that might indicate misuse of valid accounts.\n\nHowever, the control has significant limitations:\n\n1. **Default Credentials**: Training does not address the technical exploitation of publicly available default credentials on control system devices. An attacker can still use default credentials regardless of user training.\n\n2. **Credential Compromise Methods**: Many credential compromise techniques (password spraying, brute force, credential stuffing, insider threats, or lateral movement harvesting) are not meaningfully deterred by general user awareness training.\n\n3. **Persistent Attackers**: Skilled adversaries who have already obtained valid credentials through methods outside the training's scope will not be deterred by this control.\n\n4. **Coverage Scope**: While the training mentions protocols around credential sharing and physical security, it does not prevent an attacker who has already obtained valid credentials from using them for access.\n\nThe control is moderately effective only for reducing the attack surface through social engineering vectors (25-50% of potential attack vectors in a typical environment), and primarily deters less sophisticated attackers or those relying on credential theft through human manipulation. More technical credential compromise methods and post-compromise use of valid accounts remain largely unaddressed by this training-based control."}
